      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogies. Let me try to simplify it for you.\n\nWhen I said \"goblin's fist,\" I was trying to convey that the concrete is strong in compression, but brittle and prone to cracking under tensile stress. Think of it like a fist - it's compact and strong, but it's not very flexible.\n\nThe steel rebar, on the other hand, is like a network of thin, flexible wires that can absorb and distribute tensile forces. These wires are typically made of high-strength steel, which has a high tensile strength-to-weight ratio. This means that the steel rebar can withstand a lot of tensile force without breaking, while also being relatively lightweight.\n\nIn terms of physical properties, the steel rebar is typically made of a type of steel alloy that has a high yield strength (around 400-500 MPa) and a tensile strength (around 600-800 MPa). This allows it to resist both compressive and tensile forces, making it an ideal reinforcement material for concrete.\n\nAs for the \"goblin-sized\" webs, I was just trying to emphasize that the steel rebar is made up of many thin, interconnected wires that",
